1
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:04,300
An AI agent sends a payment.

2
00:00:04,300 --> 00:00:08,033
The network drops before the answer comes back.

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:10,200
Did the money move?

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,333
Across 100 trials, AgentKit could not tell. Not once.

5
00:00:14,333 --> 00:00:17,300
Through Resi, it could. 99 times.

6
00:00:17,633 --> 00:00:20,600
This is not a thought experiment.

7
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,300
Agents already pay on their own: 75 million x402 payments in 30 days.

8
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:29,267
24 million dollars, moved by software.

9
00:00:29,267 --> 00:00:33,000
Coinbase AgentKit was downloaded 40,000 times last month.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,733
Its own issue tracker already names the failure:

11
00:00:36,733 --> 00:00:42,067
a retry after a lost response sends the transfer twice. Still open.

12
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:45,533
Coinbase AgentKit ships 47 action providers.

13
00:00:45,533 --> 00:00:48,500
None of them route through KeeperHub.

14
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:52,633
Resi is that provider. One npm package, two actions.

15
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:56,700
After a transfer, three things can be true.

16
00:00:56,700 --> 00:00:58,100
It succeeded.

17
00:00:58,100 --> 00:00:59,500
It failed.

18
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:03,633
Or it went out, and the answer was lost.

19
00:01:03,633 --> 00:01:07,367
AgentKit reports that third state as a failure,

20
00:01:07,367 --> 00:01:11,500
with no id you can ever ask about again.

21
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,133
This is a real run. Receipt polling is blocked.

22
00:01:16,133 --> 00:01:19,867
The agent gets an error string. Nothing else.

23
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:23,600
So it does the reasonable thing, and retries.

24
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:26,967
On chain: two transfers, for one job.

25
00:01:26,967 --> 00:01:29,133
The money left twice.

26
00:01:29,633 --> 00:01:32,200
The same job, through Resi.

27
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:35,567
It returns an executionId, and the taskId.

28
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:38,533
The agent retries, exactly as before.

29
00:01:38,533 --> 00:01:41,533
Same executionId. Replayed, not sent again.

30
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:44,500
Then it asks what really happened,

31
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,867
and gets a receipt re-read from chain.

32
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:49,867
One transfer. Confirmed.

33
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:54,900
Last, the agent asks for 50 USDC it doesn't have.

34
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:58,267
KeeperHub simulates first, and predicts the revert.

35
00:01:58,267 --> 00:02:00,833
Nothing broadcast. No gas spent.

36
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:04,300
The model proposes: recipient, amount, taskId.

37
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,433
Code derives the idempotency key, and runs the simulation.

38
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:11,400
The model cannot switch simulation off.

39
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:14,767
KeeperHub executes once, and keeps the receipt.

40
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:19,000
One demo proves little. So we measured it.

41
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,533
100 trials per arm, the same failure, on Base Sepolia.

42
00:02:23,533 --> 00:02:26,900
AgentKit as shipped: outcome known, zero times,

43
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:30,633
and it paid twice in 47 of 47.

44
00:02:30,633 --> 00:02:34,367
Add a CDP idempotency key, the cheapest fix:

45
00:02:34,367 --> 00:02:37,733
double payments stop. Outcome known: still zero.

46
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:41,867
Through Resi: 99 of 100, and no double payments.

47
00:02:41,867 --> 00:02:44,833
AgentKit 0.9.1 gave the same answer.

48
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,633
If the money only leaves once, why know more?

49
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:53,367
Because the same error means two opposite things.

50
00:02:53,367 --> 00:02:59,067
In our runs, 99 of 400 AgentKit calls failed before anything was sent.

51
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:02,800
Expired certificates. Wallet auth errors. Real, not ours.

52
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:06,533
Under our injection, the money had already left.

53
00:03:06,533 --> 00:03:10,267
Both times, the agent read the same sentence.

54
00:03:10,267 --> 00:03:15,200
Guess it went out, and a real failure is never paid.

55
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:19,733
Guess it didn't, and without a key it pays twice.

56
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:23,467
Only an answer from chain ends the guessing.

57
00:03:25,467 --> 00:03:30,000
Last, a real model makes the calls, not a script.

58
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,367
It is asked to pay one invoice.

59
00:03:33,367 --> 00:03:37,900
The first transfer goes out, but the answer is lost.

60
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:43,233
It retries with the same taskId, and gets the same execution back.

61
00:03:43,233 --> 00:03:48,167
Then it asks what happened, and gets a receipt from chain.

62
00:03:48,167 --> 00:03:50,833
One invoice, one transfer.

63
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:55,233
The failure is induced, not sampled from production.

64
00:03:55,233 --> 00:04:00,567
The cheap fix does stop double payments. It can't say what happened.

65
00:04:00,567 --> 00:04:06,667
And it's 99, not 100: our one status call hung, and we never retried.

66
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:10,133
Don't trust these numbers. Check them.

67
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:14,267
npm run verify re-reads all 625 hashes from chain.

68
00:04:14,267 --> 00:04:16,600
625 succeeded. None missing.

69
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:19,600
And 22 tests cover the provider.

70
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:24,233
Resi. A receipt for every payment your agent makes.

71
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:27,600
Transfers only, on Base Sepolia, for now.

72
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,733
Install it, run the demo, and check every hash.
